Problem: determine the true place of the lunar node and the true inclination ρ of the lunar orbit from a total lunar eclipse, here that of 1722. Euler uses the four observed contacts: beginning, end, immersion and emersion. Section 10 on the previous page explained why: beginning and end alone give too few equations, and the magnitude of a partial eclipse cannot be observed accurately.

Model (from §11). Work on the celestial sphere with node N, inclination ρ, and the following quantities:
- x = arc from the node to the shadow centre along the ecliptic at mid-eclipse;
- y = arc from the node to the Moon's centre along its orbit;
- h = time from beginning to end; H = time from immersion to emersion;
- a = ½h(n+k), b = ½h(m+k), and A, B the same with H. These are the half-duration displacements of Moon and shadow measured from the moving node (m, n, k are the hourly motions of Sun, Moon and node);
- f = α+β and F = α−β, where α is the shadow semidiameter and β the lunar semidiameter. These are the centre distances at outer and inner contact.

In the spherical triangle (node, shadow centre, Moon centre) the spherical law of cosines gives
 cos f = cos(y∓a)cos(x∓b) + sin(y∓a)sin(x∓b)cos ρ,
and the same with F, A, B. That makes four expressions for cos ρ in the unknowns x, y, ρ.

§12. Euler argues that eliminating one unknown to get a single equation would be hopelessly complicated. Instead, since x and y are already approximately known, he applies the regula falsi in two variables. He evaluates the expressions at three trial points ('hypotheses') and, implicitly, interpolates linearly to the corrections p, q that make the cos ρ values agree.

Numerical data for 1722: f = 1°1'17", F = 28'2", a = 1°2'22", b = 4'11", A = 19'48", B = 1'20", with first approximations x = 4°16'43" and y = x − c = 4°15'28". The initial hypotheses perturb y and x separately by 10'.

§13. For logarithmic computation he converts to half-angle form. From 1 − cos ρ = [cos(u−v) − cos f]/(sin u sin v) with u = y−a, v = x−b one gets
 sin²½ρ = −sin½(x−y+a−b−f)·sin½(x−y+a−b+f) / [sin(y−a)sin(x−b)],
and the analogues II–IV. He tabulates a−b, a−b±f and their halves, and likewise A−B, A−B±F and their halves.

§14. He changes the parametrization ('alias hypotheses fingi conveniet') to ½(x−y) and ½(x+y):
- I: (38", 4°16'0");
- II: (38", 4°11'0");
- III: (28", 4°16'0").
The true values are written 38" − p and 4°16'0" − q. The next page evaluates all four formulas at the three hypotheses. The result is p ≈ 102", hence y > x, which is physically inadmissible. Euler attributes this to the atmosphere enlarging the shadow. He estimates the enlargement from twilight as 1/71, i.e. 38" of 44'40", and eventually adopts about 30". He concludes that eclipses cannot improve the tabular node and inclination.
